{{nihongo|'''Mount Asuka'''|浅香山|Asuka-yama}} is an active stratovolcano situated on the central industrial island of Ishijima within the [[Okaiken]]. Standing at a summit elevation of 4000 meters, it is the undisputed highest mountain in the country and serves as a primary cultural icon for the Sanese people. The mountain is recognized for its exceptionally symmetrical cone, which is covered in snow for several months of the year, dominating the skyline of the central provinces. As a highly active complex volcano, it sits within the volcanic range that encompasses the Shinano Caldera and is closely monitored by the Ministry of Technology.

==Geography and Climate==
Mount Asuka is a distinctive feature of the Okaiken geography, located near the eastern coast of central Ishijima. It is surrounded by the Five Lakes of Shinano, which provide mirrored views of its symmetrical slopes. The summit experiences a tundra climate characterized by low temperatures and high wind speeds, while the lower slopes are dominated by dense forests of blue pine and cedar. Seasonal snowcaps typically form in early October and persist until late May, creating a stark white peak that is visible from the capital city of Okami on clear days. The base of the mountain serves as a crucial watershed for the industrial sectors of Ishijima, feeding the rivers that power the regional hydroelectric grids.

==Geology==
The geological structure of Mount Asuka is defined by its position at a volatile tectonic junction within the Ishijima Volcanic Range. It is primarily composed of non-alkali mafic and pyroclastic volcanic rocks, with andesite and dacite being the dominant rock types. Advanced scientific research conducted by the Ministry of Technology utilizes muon radiography to image the internal cavities of the volcano, providing a detailed understanding of the density structures below the crater floor. Pre-eruptive degassing and magma movements are continuously analyzed through the Satori predictive algorithm suite to maintain national safety and operational continuity for the nearby industrial hubs.

==Eruptive history==
The eruptive history of Mount Asuka is one of the most thoroughly documented in Sanese records, characterized by a pattern of frequent and occasionally catastrophic activity that has shaped the Ishijima landscape. It is officially classified among the volcanoes that have erupted disastrously during the past century. One of the earliest significant events recorded in imperial chronicles is the plinian eruption of 1108, which was twice as large in magnitude as subsequent centuries' events. This eruption blanketed the Ishijima plains in a thick layer of ash, rendering rice paddies uncultivable and contributing to severe regional climate disturbances. A later climactic event occurred in 1783, lasting for fifteen hours and producing massive pyroclastic flows and andesitic pumice falls. This eruption significantly enlarged the primary cone and resulted in the formation of the Oni-no-Iwa or Demon's Rock lava field on the northern slope, which remains a prominent geological feature and tourist destination today.

Beyond these major catastrophes, Mount Asuka has maintained a consistent record of explosive and minor eruptions that mark the span of Sanese history. In the early 20th century, activity was particularly dense, with eruptions recorded in 1900, 1904, 1908, and throughout the years leading up to 1914. Significant explosive events occurred throughout the 1920s and the mid-century period. The period following the Great Adonian War saw no cessation in activity, with eruptions noted frequently between 1944 and 1973. In the late 20th century, explosive eruptions in 1982 and 1983 sent fine ash as far as the capital city of Okami, and more than a thousand earthquakes were detected at the mountain in 1995. Recent activity has included a single vulcanian eruption in 2004 that caused regional fires, followed by ash eruptions in 2008 and 2009. The most recent activity was observed on August 7, 2019, when a small eruption emitted a smoke plume reaching 1800 meters above the mountain, prompting the Satori network to raise the local alert level.

==Cultural Significance==
Mount Asuka holds a sacred position in the Shoto religious system as the Goddess of the High Peak, often associated with the sun goddess Amadera. Shrines located at the foothills serve as centers for mountain asceticism and sites where worshippers offer prayers for national '''pinnacle''' and protection from seismic events. The mountain's silhouette is a favorite theme in Sanese decorative art and has inspired countless poets and filmmakers throughout the imperial and republican eras. It is also celebrated in the national anthem, Furusato, where its presence serves as a reminder of the enduring connection between the Sanese people and their rugged archipelago.

